    <item>
      <title>The Birthday Paradox in Production: When Random IDs Collide</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/birthday-paradox/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 24 Nov 2025 06:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/birthday-paradox/</guid>
      <description>&lt;figure style=&#34;float: right; margin: 0 20px 10px 20px; width: 250px; text-align: center;&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;img src=&#34;./birthday-paradox.png&#34; alt=&#34;Multiple database servers with overlapping ID bubbles showing collision zones&#34; width=&#34;250&#34; style=&#34;display: block; margin: 0 auto;&#34;&gt;&#xA;  &lt;figcaption style=&#34;font-size: 0.9em; color: #555; margin-top: 5px;&#34;&gt;&#xA;    &lt;em&gt;With just 23 random selections from 365 options, you have a 50% chance of collision. Now imagine billions of UUIDs.&lt;/em&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You generate a UUID. It&amp;rsquo;s 128 bits of randomness—340 undecillion possible values. Collision-proof, right? Your system generates a million IDs per second. Still safe? What about a billion?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;In a room of 23 people, there&amp;rsquo;s a greater than 50% chance two share a birthday. In your database, collisions arrive far sooner than intuition suggests.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;</description>
    </item>
